> 6 Dinan         2017 <NA>                         Hurricanes              2017   1.4 None       <NA>      0.16    NA             NA       NA                 NA        NA    2025 (any)      
> ```
> 
> The other looks identical, but all of its Impact, ..., `High Value` columns are inferred. I want to now combine them, so that I get all of these rows, plus any rows from the inferred data frame where the Bucket column is not present in this "real values" dataset. How do I do that?
**assistant**:
> Use an `anti_join()` to retain only inferred rows whose `Bucket` does not occur in the real-data table, then append those to the real data.
> 
> ```r
> library(dplyr)
> 
> combined_df <- bind_rows(
>   df,
>   inferred_df %>%
>     anti_join(df %>% distinct(Bucket), by = "Bucket")
> )
> ```
> 
> This gives you:
> 
> - every row in `df` (the real values); and
> - rows from `inferred_df` only for `Bucket`s entirely absent from `df`.
> 
> If `Bucket` is only unique *within* other identifiers—for example, an `Outcome` may have the same Bucket as another Outcome—join on the full identifying key instead:
> 
> ```r
> id_cols <- c("Author(s)", "Year", "DOI", "Outcome",
>              "Time Period", "GSAT", "Adaptation", "Region", "Bucket")
> 
> combined_df <- bind_rows(
>   df,
>   inferred_df %>%
>     anti_join(df %>% distinct(across(all_of(id_cols))), by = id_cols)
> )
> ```
> 
> That second approach is generally safer if `Bucket` labels such as `"2050 1.3 - 1.7 C"` recur for different outcomes or studies.
